From f393072cfbc6eaf061412575ca016cefc8b73dd3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: nsbradley88 <3960699+nsbradley88@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 04:50:06 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 21/46] llama: Quest-style block-sparse decode attention
 (LLAMA_SPARSE_ATTN)

LLAMA_SPARSE_ATTN=k,bs,min_ctx[,sink,recent] (non-SWA layers of a single-stream unified cache, no MLA):
decode ubatches (<= 8 tokens) at n_kv >= min_ctx attend only to the top-k blocks of bs cells per token.

- Per block and KV head the cache keeps the elementwise max and min of the keys (ks, F32
  [2*hd, kv_size/bs, n_head_kv]). The bound relu(q).max - relu(-q).min is one mul_mat; per-head softmax
  over blocks, summed over heads, with sink (first 128 tokens) and recent (last 1024) blocks forced and
  empty blocks excluded, then top_k. The selection becomes the attention mask and FA runs with the
  n_kv_max sparse hint.
- The bounds are refreshed on the host after each ubatch (llama_kv_cache::sparse_host_update): prompt
  ubatches refresh every block they touched, decode only a block whose last cell was written (a partly
  written block is always among the forced recent blocks). Refreshing them inside the forward graph on
  Vulkan corrupted the forward pass at depth (single-token NaN out of a full-attention layer past ~30K),
  which no backend switch (fusion, graph optimize, barriers, submit batching) avoided.
- The selection bias input scans only sink and tail blocks per token, keeping the host cost independent
  of n_kv; graph reuse checks the sparse/dense choice and block count.

Laguna-S-2.1, q8_0 KV, Strix Halo, LLAMA_SPARSE_ATTN=64,64,16384: decode 19.5 -> 26.4 tok/s at 128K,
15.0 -> 24.2 at 245K; needle 15/15 at 81K; greedy output at 128K equivalent to dense.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
